  "live_records": {
    "q-277052": {
      "dom_id": "step-q277052",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=1-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=1-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=1-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{y} & =1-x \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =2(1-x) \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(2(1-x))}^{3} \\\\ y & =8(1-x)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=8(1-x)^{3}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
