    "q-148429": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148429",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=4\\sqrt{x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=4\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=4\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{1}{4}x \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{4}x)}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0,\\,f(x)\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 0.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-148429-a",
