    "q-148457": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148457",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{x}}-4.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{x}}-4\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{y}}-4.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{y}}-4 \\\\ x+4 & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+4} & =\\frac{3\\sqrt[3]{y}}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{2}{3(x+4)} \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(\\frac{2}{3(x+4)})}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{8}{27(x+4)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{8}{27(x+4)^{3}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,f(x)\\ne -4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne -4,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne 0.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-148457-a",
