    "q-277814": {
      "dom_id": "step-q277814",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=2-4\\sqrt{x+3}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2-4\\sqrt{x+3}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=2-4\\sqrt{y+3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2-4\\sqrt{y+3} \\\\ 4\\sqrt{y+3} & =2-x \\\\ \\sqrt{y+3} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2-x) \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y+3})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{4}(2-x))}^{2} \\\\ y+3 & =\\frac{1}{16}(2-x)^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{16}(2-x)^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{16}(2-x)^{2}-3.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge -3,\\,f(x)\\le 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\le 2,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge -3.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
