    "q-148542": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148542",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{4x+1}-2.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{4x+1}-2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{4y+1}-2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt[3]{4y+1}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{4y+1} & =x+2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{4y+1})}^{3} & ={(x+2)}^{3} \\\\ 4y+1 & =(x+2)^{3} \\\\ 4y & =(x+2)^{3}-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}(x+2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-148542-a",
