    "q-270702": {
      "dom_id": "step-q270702",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2y+1}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2y+1}} \\\\ \\sqrt{2y+1} & =\\frac{5}{x} \\\\ (\\sqrt{2y+1})^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{x})}^{2} \\\\ 2y+1 & =\\frac{25}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2y & =\\frac{25}{{x}^{2}}-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{25}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{25}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>-\\frac{1}{2},\\,f(x)>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>0,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$",
