    "q-148569": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148569",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{kx+2}-2$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}(x+2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}.$ What is the value of $k?$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{kx+2}-2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{ky+2}-2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt[3]{ky+2}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{ky+2} & =x+2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{ky+2})}^{3} & ={(x+2)}^{3} \\\\ ky+2 & =(x+2)^{3} \\\\ ky & =(x+2)^{3}-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{k}(x+2)^{3}-\\frac{2}{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{k}(x+2)^{3}-\\frac{2}{k}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{k}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,k=4.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
